{
  "id": "zowppi0",
  "title": "Chapter 9: Chemical Reactions",
  "order": 58,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ch3llua",
      "content": "Chemical reactions are processes in which substances undergo chemical changes to form new substances with different properties. During a chemical reaction, atoms rearrange themselves to form new molecules. Chemical reactions are essential in everyday life, industrial processes and biological systems."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Main Reaction Types",
          "value": "5 Primary Types",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Governing Principle",
          "value": "Conservation of Mass",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Water Electrolysis Ratio",
          "value": "2:1 (H2 : O2)",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Key Cause of Rancidity",
          "value": "Lipid Oxidation",
          "icon": "clock"
        }
      ],
      "id": "mirujof"
    },
    {
      "id": "c245qw7",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning of Chemical Reaction"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Chemical reactions involve the breaking and forming of chemical bonds between atoms to produce new substances with entirely different properties.",
        "A balanced chemical equation must have an equal number of atoms of each element on both sides to satisfy the Law of Conservation of Mass.",
        "Key indicators of a chemical reaction include temperature change, evolution of a gas, color change, and formation of a precipitate.",
        "Oxidation is defined as the gain of oxygen or loss of hydrogen/electrons, whereas reduction is the loss of oxygen or gain of hydrogen/electrons.",
        "Exothermic reactions release thermal energy to the surroundings, whereas endothermic reactions absorb heat energy."
      ],
      "id": "3yzbrr5"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3tmwejo",
      "content": "• A chemical reaction is a process in which one or more substances are transformed into new substances."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "io0b1ve",
      "content": "• The substances that take part in a reaction are called reactants."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xntrg6s",
      "content": "• The substances formed in a reaction are called products."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ov6raj5",
      "content": "• Chemical reactions involve breaking and forming of chemical bonds."
    },
    {
      "id": "cmh64ka",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Chemical Equation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g1joqk4",
      "content": "A chemical equation is a symbolic representation of a chemical reaction using chemical formulas."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "iykjv9g",
      "content": "Example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "blaaae0",
      "content": "2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O"
    },
    {
      "id": "d1kohw1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Balanced Chemical Equation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "c8x4rxj",
      "content": "A balanced chemical equation has an equal number of atoms of each element on both sides."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ng7ycun",
      "content": "Example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1eqt12n",
      "content": "CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O"
    },
    {
      "id": "01ubhxo",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Indicators of Chemical Reaction"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "A balanced chemical equation must have an equal number of atoms of each element on both sides of the equation to satisfy the Law of Conservation of Mass.",
      "id": "d41z6wu"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "bc3bzow",
      "content": "• Change in colour"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "aih31pd",
      "content": "• Change in temperature"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "40ghypj",
      "content": "• Formation of gas"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "adctdbk",
      "content": "• Formation of precipitate"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gdnpqw2",
      "content": "• Emission of light or sound"
    },
    {
      "id": "3w79rmd",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Types of Chemical Reactions"
    },
    {
      "id": "ki58d6x",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Reaction Type</strong></p>",
